<p>A customer asks what a wall, a slab or a roof will cost. The material calculator works out the materials from the
  measurements and prices them at your shelf prices, and one press makes it a quote. Find it under
  <strong>Sell › Orders & quotes</strong> (the actions menu beside the title) or from <strong>New quote</strong>.</p>

<h2>Before the first time: link the materials</h2>
<p>Someone who looks after the catalogue opens <strong>Link materials to products</strong> and, for each material (cement,
  sand, ballast, stones and blocks, Y8 to Y16 bars, BRC mesh, binding wire, iron sheets, ridge caps, roofing nails, tiles,
  adhesive, grout, paint), types the SKU, barcode or name of the product you sell for it. If you sell it in a different unit,
  say how many of your product one unit of the material is: <strong>1000</strong> for sand sold by the kg (the calculator
  works in tonnes), <strong>0.04</strong> for binding wire sold in 25 kg rolls, <strong>0.05</strong> for paint sold in
  20-litre tins. A material that isn't linked is still worked out, but left off the total and the quote.</p>

<h2>Measuring the job</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Wall</strong>: length and height (all the walls together), the area of doors and windows to leave out, what it's
    built with (9×9 or 6×9 machine-cut stone, 6 or 4 inch blocks) and the mortar mix.</li>
  <li><strong>Concrete</strong>: a slab, footings or a beam, with how many alike, the mix (1:2:4 for slabs and beams), and the steel:
    BRC mesh, or bars at a spacing both ways.</li>
  <li><strong>Plastering</strong>: the area (both faces of a wall count) and thickness.</li>
  <li><strong>Roofing</strong>: the roof's length along the ridge and from eave to ridge, one or two sides, and the sheet length.</li>
  <li><strong>Tiling</strong> and <strong>Painting</strong>: the area, and for paint the coats and what the tin says it covers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Add as many parts as the job has (outside walls and inside walls as two walls, say), press <strong>Work it out</strong>, and
  the materials and their total appear beside them. Each part says what it assumed: the joints, the waste, the overlaps.</p>

<h2>Making the quote</h2>
<p>Choose the customer and press <strong>Make a quote</strong>. The quote has one line per material at the customer's prices,
  rounded up to whole bags, lengths and sheets, and its note lists what was measured. Change anything you like before sending it;
  from there it's an ordinary quote, confirmed into an order, paid a deposit and collected at the till.</p>
<p class="hint">The figures are a counter's estimate, not an engineer's. For a large job, the customer's builder or quantity
  surveyor has the last word.</p>
